    39	## What the issuer does (revision 3, after `CODEX-CONFIRM.md` ILC-01 to ILC-05)
    40	
    41	The second revision let any worker latch or expire, and relied on compare-and-set plus re-observation. Codex found
    42	interleavings in which a stale or partial fetch expires an attempt that another, stalled worker had already observed
    43	on time (ILC-01). Compare-and-set makes the writes exclusive. It does not make the earlier observation win. Revision 3
    44	removes the concurrency instead of arbitrating it.
    45	
    46	1. **One writer.** Every lifecycle transition (start an attempt, latch, discard, expire, refuse, complete) is made
    47	   only by the poller, which runs with reserved concurrency 1 (`service/issuer/infra/main.tf:354`). The API is
    48	   read-only for the lifecycle: `api.status` no longer calls `settle` (`api.py:194`). Every transition is a write
    49	   guarded on the expected state *and* attempt id, so a write meant for an ended attempt fails (ILC-05).
    50	2. **Attempt isolation.** As before: each attempt's declared time, frames, signature jobs, proofs and evidence live
    51	   under its id. A late response names its attempt and can never fill another.
    52	3. **Latch.** On each poll, for each open attempt, the poller fetches and upgrades the attempt's evidence and
    53	   evaluates the three-conjunct predicate with k-deep burial (see "What is registered"). If it holds, the poller
    54	   writes the latch with `observed_at` (a wall reading taken after the evidence is in hand) and the digests of the
    55	   qualifying presentation, which is kept. The observation counts once that write is durable. A latch may be written
    56	   at any time before the attempt expires: the A2.1 "may" is exercised permissively.
    57	4. **Expiry.** An attempt expires only when all of these hold:
    58	   - (a) it is not latched, as read by the poller in this invocation;
    59	   - (b) the wall reading, taken after that read, is past `B + WRITE_LEASE_S`. B itself is never moved earlier;
    60	     waiting the lease longer only lets any latch write from a killed earlier invocation (begun with
    61	     `observed_at ≤ B`) commit first, under the stated write-duration assumption
    62	     (`docs/decisions/2026-10-27-service-clock-tolerance.md`);
    63	   - (c) this invocation's own fresh fetch, from every calendar the attempt was stamped to and the configured
    64	     explorers, does not establish eligibility.
    65	
    66	   Expiry is permitted strictly after B, so expiring later than B is allowed. What remains is a stated residual: an
    67	   on-time observation that was never made durable (its invocation was killed before the write) is lost if the
    68	   evidence sources then regress, for example during a calendar or explorer outage. Evidence otherwise only
    69	   accumulates (calendars do not retract commitments, and burial deepens), so a later fetch sees at least what an
    70	   earlier one did.
    71	5. **Unavailable evidence.** If (c) cannot be evaluated because a source is unreachable, the attempt is not expired
    72	   on that poll. After an operating period past B (runbook), an *unlatched* attempt whose evidence is still
    73	   unobtainable is ended with reason `EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE`. A latched attempt is never ended for unavailability.
    74	   This is how termination is kept as an obligation on the implementation (A2.3) without inventing a hard bound.
    75	6. **Permanent failure (discard before B).** A conjunct counts as permanently failed only when *every* proof for the
    76	   attempt is complete (no pending calendar), so the lowest qualifying height, and with it T and the confirmation
    77	   segment, can no longer change, and that presentation fails a conjunct. Otherwise the attempt waits (ILC-02).
    78	7. **After the latch.** The preserved qualifying presentation is used for assembly; it is never swapped for a
    79	   different, worse one. A transient failure (store, explorer, calendar, DNS snapshot) is retried. The attempt is
    80	   discarded only for a failure the frozen attempt cannot cure even with recovered evidence: T of the preserved
    81	   presentation > not-after (A9 `:422`), or a verifier reason that depends only on the attempt's committed bytes.
    82	   Each discard records its reason (ILC-04).
    83	8. **Next attempt and refusal.** Ending attempt i and allocating attempt i+1 happen in one guarded write. So a crash
